@using System.IO;
@using System.Net.Http
@using System.Net.Http.Json
@using System.Text.Json
@using System.Text
@using System.Threading.Tasks
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.Http
@using System.Runtime.InteropServices.JavaScript;

@using GdsII;
@using GDSViewer.Models;
@using GDSViewer.Components;

@*
    The app's one route. The view is carried in the query string rather than as a path segment, because
    index.html sets <base href> from window.location.pathname so that publishing to a subdirectory needs
    no rebuild - which means any path below the app root would become the base and break every relative
    asset URL and the import map with it. A query string leaves the path alone.

    It also avoids needing the host to rewrite unknown paths onto index.html, which GitHub Pages does not
    do, so a shared link keeps working wherever this is served from.
*@
@page "/"

@inject HttpClient httpClient
@inject IJSRuntime js;
@inject NavigationManager NavigationManager
@inject AppStorage storage
@inject HistoryStore history


<PageTitle>GDSII Viewer</PageTitle>


@*
    Every wrapper between the page and the view is a flex column that gives its height away, so the view
    ends up with whatever the window has left after the toolbar and the padding.

    They are all here rather than one rule on the view, because a flex height only travels as far as the
    next element that will not pass it on: one ancestor sized to its own contents and the chain stops
    there. That is what left the app taller than the window and put a scrollbar on the page.
*@
<div class="viewerPage @(fullScreen ? "viewerPageFull" : "")">
    <!-- *********************************************** Main App ***************************************************** -->

    <div class="row viewerPageRow" style="justify-content: center;">
        @*
            data-file names whatever is open. The picker used to be on screen at all times and was the
            answer to "which file is this?"; behind a button it is not, and nothing else on the page says
            so in every view - the layer sidebar is absent in the text view, and the canvas says nothing.
        *@
        <div id="mainAppContainer" class="col" data-file="@(CurrentlySelectedFileName + CurrentlySelectedFileType)">

            @*
                There is no separate top toolbar any more. Everything it held is either in the view's own
                bar below - open, save and share, which act on the file and are reached for constantly - or
                behind the Examples button there, which is where the bundled layouts and the PDK links
                went. One bar rather than two: the second one cost a row of screen to carry three controls,
                and the file picker was the only one of them anybody used twice.
            *@

            <!-- *********************************************** View Change ***************************************************** -->

            @*
                The bar's own padding lives here rather than on each control in it. Every toolbarItem
                carried 10px all round, so the row's height was however tall the tallest control's padding
                made it and the gap above the first item was set by something three components away.
            *@
            @*
                The bar, unless the page asked for the canvas on its own.

                Not merely hidden: left out of the render entirely, so nothing in it is focusable, reachable
                by keyboard, or read out - a toolbar that is invisible and still tabbable is worse than one
                that is there.
            *@
            @if (!viewerOnly)
            {
            <div class="row viewBorderBackground" style="border-bottom: none;">
                 <div class="row viewToolbar">

                     @*
                         What acts on the file, ahead of everything the view contributes. The pair shares
                         one column rather than taking one each: two small icons that read as in and out
                         together, the same way the layer sidebar pairs its own two.
                     *@
                     @*
                         Buttons rather than pictures on the page.

                         They were two PNGs with a cursor over them - the only controls in the bar that were
                         images rather than buttons, and the only ones that did not look pressable. Blue
                         squares with white glyphs, the same as everything else up here, drawn inline so
                         they inherit the color and stay sharp at any zoom.

                         The label keeps its `for`, which is the whole reason a file input is reachable at
                         all: the input itself is hidden and unstyleable, and the label is what stands in
                         for it. Styled as a button, it is one everywhere but in the markup.
                     *@
                     @*
                         No fence after these. The two lists beside them open files too, so the four of them
                         are one group with an ordinary gutter between its halves - the fence belongs past
                         Examples, where the controls stop being about getting a file on screen.
                     *@
                     <div class="col-1 toolbarItem" style="align-self: end; display: flex; gap: 0.4em;">
                         @*
                             The dialog opens on layouts rather than on everything in the folder.

                             Names only - the reader decides what a file is by looking inside it, not by
                             what it is called, so this is the picker's filter and not the app's. A file
                             that arrives some other way is read on its contents exactly as before.

                             .dxf is in the list because the app opens those too. Leaving it out would hide
                             a format that works from the only dialog most people use to reach it.
                         *@
                         <InputFile id="fileUpload" style="display: none;" OnChange="onFileInputChanged" multiple
                                    accept=".gds,.gds2,.gdsii,.oas,.oasis,.dxf" />

                         @*
                             Turned off rather than taken away, so the bar keeps its shape and somebody can
                             see that opening a file is a thing this does - it is the page they are on that
                             has said no, not the app that lacks it.

                             A label rather than a button, so `disabled` means nothing to it: what stops the
                             click is pointer-events, and the class carries both that and the look.
                         *@
                         <label for="@(noEditing ? "" : "fileUpload")"
                                class="buttonStyle2 toolbarButton buttonIcon fileButton @(noEditing ? "toolbarButtonOff" : "")"
                                aria-disabled="@(noEditing ? "true" : "false")"
                                title="@(noEditing ? "This viewer is read-only - opening a file is turned off on this page" : "Open a GDS, OASIS or DXF file from this computer")">
                             @*Into the tray, which is the way round every file dialog draws it.*@
                             <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true" role="img" aria-label="Open a file">
                                 <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"
                                       stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                       d="M8 10.6V1.8M4.8 5 8 1.8 11.2 5M2.2 10.4v2.6a1.2 1.2 0 0 0 1.2 1.2h9.2a1.2 1.2 0 0 0 1.2-1.2v-2.6" />
                             </svg>
                         </label>

                         @*
                             The download and the format it writes, as one control.

                             They were a button and a dropdown with a gap between them, which reads as two
                             things that happen to be adjacent - and one of them makes no sense without the
                             other: a format that nothing writes, and a save with no say in what it writes.
                             Joined, the way the grid's icon is joined to its pitch.
                         *@
                         <div class="joinedPair">
                             <button id="downloadGds" class="buttonStyle2 toolbarButton buttonIcon fileButton joinedStart"
                                     @onclick="downloadFile"
                                     aria-label="Save the file"
                                     title="Save the file as it currently stands, edits included">
                                 <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true">
                                     <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"
                                           stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                           d="M8 1.8v8.8M4.8 7.4 8 10.6l3.2-3.2M2.2 10.4v2.6a1.2 1.2 0 0 0 1.2 1.2h9.2a1.2 1.2 0 0 0 1.2-1.2v-2.6" />
                                 </svg>
                             </button>

                             @*
                                 What the download writes, joined to the button that writes it. Starts on
                                 whatever the file arrived as, since converting is the exception and keeping
                                 the format is the rule - and the two are the same click either way.

                                 value plus @onchange rather than @bind, so the session hears about it.
                             *@
                             <select id="downloadFormat" class="formatPicker joinedEnd" value="@downloadFormat" @onchange="changeDownloadFormat"
                                     title="Which format the download button beside this writes">
                                 <option value=".gds">GDS</option>
                                 <option value=".oas">OASIS</option>
                                 <option value=".dxf">DXF</option>
                             </select>
                         </div>

                     </div>

                     @*
                         The two lists of files, History first.

                         **Both open a file, so they belong together**, and the order is which one is nearer
                         the controls that also open files: History is a list of what you have had open on
                         this computer, Examples is what the app came with. Close enough together to read as
                         a pair, and one ordinary column gutter from the Open and Save buttons rather than a
                         group fence - the four of them are all about getting a file on screen, and the fence
                         belongs after them, before the controls that are about looking at it.

                         History is a clock, which is the icon everybody already knows for "what did I have
                         open before". Nothing draws "examples", so that one stays a word - at the size every
                         other button in the bar is, since the stack that made it a size smaller is gone.
                     *@
                     @*
                         **The two lists hang off these two buttons**, and go when the pointer does.

                         They were dialogs in the middle of the window with a cross in the corner, which is
                         a lot of ceremony for "what else could I open" - a question usually answered by
                         looking rather than by choosing. Pointing at the button is enough to ask it and
                         moving away is enough to stop, so there is nothing to dismiss and no cross.

                         The host is the column rather than each button, so the two lists share one place to
                         hang from and one leave to close on: the pointer crossing from History to Examples
                         never leaves the host, which is what stops the list flickering as it passes.
                     *@
                     <div class="col-1 toolbarItem toolbarGroupEnd toolbarPopupHost" style="align-self: end; display: flex; gap: 0.4em; align-items: end;"
                          @onmouseleave="closeLists">
                         @*One at a time: they are both popups over the same corner of the page.*@
                         @*
                             Off in a read-only embed. The history is a record of what has been opened on
                             this machine, and a page showing somebody else's layout has no business
                             offering that to whoever is reading it.
                         *@
                         <button id="historyButton" class="buttonStyle2 toolbarButton buttonIcon fileButton"
                                 @onclick="openHistory" @onmouseenter="openHistory"
                                 disabled="@noEditing"
                                 aria-label="History"
                                 title="@(noEditing ? "This viewer is read-only - the file history is turned off on this page" : "Files you have opened from this computer or changed, as you left them")">
                             @*
                                 Anticlockwise: the hands going back is what makes it history rather than a
                                 clock, and the arrow is the only part of the drawing carrying that.
                             *@
                             <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true">
                                 <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"
                                       stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                       d="M2.2 8a5.8 5.8 0 1 1 1.7 4.1" />
                                 <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"
                                       stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                       d="M1.5 5.1 2.2 8.4 5.5 7.7" />
                                 <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"
                                       stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                       d="M8 4.8V8l2.3 1.4" />
                             </svg>
                         </button>

                         <button id="examplesButton" class="buttonStyle2 toolbarButton fileButton examplesButton"
                                 @onclick="openExamples" @onmouseenter="openExamples"
                                 title="The bundled example layouts, and links to the PDK they come from">Examples</button>

                         @*
                             Both lists live here, under the buttons that open them.

                             Rendered inside the host rather than at the foot of the page: an absolutely
                             positioned child is still a descendant, so the pointer moving from the button
                             onto the list never leaves the host and the mouseleave above does not fire.
                             Laid out at the page's end, they would have been a separate thing to leave.
                         *@
                         @if (showHistory)
                         {
                            <div class="popupDiv popupZIndex_0 popupUnder">
                                <div class="col">

                                    @*The same heading and cross as Examples; see the note there.*@
                                    <div class="popupHeading">
                                        <span class="popupHeadingName">History</span>

                                        <button id="closeHistory" class="buttonStyle2 closeButton"
                                                @onclick="hideExamples"
                                                aria-label="Close" title="Close">&times;</button>
                                    </div>

                                    <hr class="popupDivider" />

                                    <div class="row mb-3">
                                        <div class="examplePicker">
                                            <div class="examplePickerList">
                                                @*
                                                    The same filter as the example list has. This one is capped at twenty
                                                    rather than nine hundred, so it is not the difference between usable
                                                    and hopeless here - but the names in it are long and near-identical
                                                    for a run of cells out of one library, which is the case it is for.
                                                *@
                                                <input type="text"
                                                       class="examplePickerFilter historyPickerFilter"
                                                       value="@historyFilter"
                                                       placeholder="Filter @historyEntries.Count file@(historyEntries.Count == 1 ? "" : "s")"
                                                       title="Narrow the list. Matches anywhere in the name"
                                                       @oninput="onHistoryFilterInput" />

                                                <div id="historyPicker" class="examplePickerOptions" role="listbox"
                                                     @onmouseleave="showSelectedPreview">
                                                    @if (historyEntries.Count == 0)
                                                    {
                                                        @*
                                                            Says what puts something here, rather than only that nothing
                                                            is. The rule is not guessable from an empty box: an example
                                                            you only looked at is deliberately not kept.
                                                        *@
                                                        <div class="examplePickerNone">
                                                            Nothing yet. Files you open from this computer are kept here, and so is any file you change.
                                                        </div>
                                                    }
                                                    else if (filteredHistory.Count == 0)
                                                    {
                                                        <div class="examplePickerNone">Nothing matches "@historyFilter"</div>
                                                    }
                                                    else
                                                    {
                                                        @*
                                                            Not virtualized, unlike the example list. That one is nine
                                                            hundred rows; this one is capped at twenty, and Virtualize
                                                            would only put a scroll container around rows that all fit.
                                                        *@
                                                        @foreach (var entry in filteredHistory)
                                                        {
                                                            <div class="examplePickerOption historyRow @(entry.Name == CurrentlySelectedFileName + CurrentlySelectedFileType ? "isChosen" : "")"
                                                                 role="option"
                                                                 aria-selected="@((entry.Name == CurrentlySelectedFileName + CurrentlySelectedFileType).ToString().ToLowerInvariant())"
                                                                 data-file="@entry.Name"
                                                                 @onmouseenter="() => historyPreviewOnHover(entry.Name)">

                                                                @*
                                                                    The name is what opens the row, rather than the row
                                                                    itself: a click anywhere on the row would also be a
                                                                    click on the delete button sitting in it, and
                                                                    deleting a file would then open it on the way out.
                                                                *@
                                                                <span class="historyRowName" title="@rowTitle(entry)" @onclick="() => openFromHistory(entry.Name)">@entry.Name</span>

                                                                @if (entry.Edited)
                                                                {
                                                                    <span class="historyRowEdited" title="The records in this file were changed here">edited</span>
                                                                }

                                                                <button class="historyRowForget" @onclick="() => forgetFromHistory(entry.Name)"
                                                                        title="Forget @entry.Name">&times;</button>
                                                            </div>
                                                        }
                                                    }
                                                </div>
                                            </div>

                                            @*The same frame as the example picker's, drawn from the same preview code.*@
                                            <div class="examplePreviewFrame">
                                                @if (previewMarkup.Length > 0)
                                                {
                                                    <svg class="examplePreview" viewBox="@previewViewBox"
                                                         xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                                                         role="img" aria-label="Preview of @(historyPreviewFor.Length > 0 ? historyPreviewFor : CurrentlySelectedFileName)">
                                                        @((MarkupString)previewMarkup)
                                                    </svg>
                                                }
                                                else
                                                {
                                                    <div class="examplePreviewEmpty">No preview</div>
                                                }
                                            </div>
                                        </div>
                                    </div>

                                    @*
                                        Under the list rather than up beside the close button.

                                        Emptying the whole list is the one thing in this popup that cannot be undone,
                                        and up in the heading it sat next to the control people reach for to dismiss a
                                        dialog without reading it. Down here it is where you arrive after looking at
                                        what you would be throwing away.

                                        Only offered when there is something to clear, so it is not a permanent
                                        invitation to press something that would do nothing.
                                    *@
                                    @if (historyEntries.Count > 0)
                                    {
                                        <div class="row">
                                            <div class="col d-flex justify-content-start">
                                                <button id="clearHistoryButton" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton" @onclick="clearHistory"
                                                        title="Forget every file listed here. The files on your computer are untouched">Clear History</button>
                                            </div>
                                        </div>
                                    }

                                </div>
                            </div>
                         }

                         @if (showExamples)
                         {
                            <div class="popupDiv popupZIndex_0 popupUnder">
                                <div class="col">

                                    @*
                                        The heading, and a cross.

                                        **The cross came back.** This popup closes when the pointer leaves
                                        it, which is quick once you know it and is nothing at all if you do
                                        not: there is no control to look for, so the way out has to be
                                        discovered by accident. Both now - the move still works, and the
                                        cross is there for anybody who reaches for one.

                                        A flex row rather than two bootstrap columns. Two `col`s split the
                                        width in half and the heading took the left one, which left a band
                                        of empty column across the top of the popup wide enough to read as
                                        a missing control.
                                    *@
                                    <div class="popupHeading">
                                        <span class="popupHeadingName">Examples</span>

                                        <button id="closeExamples" class="buttonStyle2 closeButton"
                                                @onclick="hideExamples"
                                                aria-label="Close" title="Close">&times;</button>
                                    </div>

                                    @*Bracketing the heading off from the body, the same way About does.*@
                                    <hr class="popupDivider" />

                                    <div class="row mb-3">
                                        <div class="examplePicker">
                                            @*
                                                Rows rather than a select, so pointing at one can show it.

                                                An <option> is drawn by the operating system and reports nothing back
                                                to the page - no hover, no focus - so a native picker could only ever
                                                preview what had already been chosen. These are divs, so they can.

                                                Virtualized, because there are nine hundred of them and rendering that
                                                many on every keystroke in the filter box is not free. Filtered too,
                                                which is what makes a list this long usable at all: the names differ by
                                                a few characters at the end, so scrolling for one is hopeless.
                                            *@
                                            <div class="examplePickerList">
                                                <input type="text"
                                                       class="examplePickerFilter"
                                                       value="@exampleFilter"
                                                       placeholder="Filter @((exampleFiles.Count + 1).ToString()) examples"
                                                       title="Narrow the list. Matches anywhere in the name"
                                                       @oninput="onExampleFilterInput" />

                                                <div id="examplePicker" class="examplePickerOptions" role="listbox"
                                                     @onmouseleave="showSelectedPreview">
                                                    @if (filteredExamples.Count == 0)
                                                    {
                                                        <div class="examplePickerNone">Nothing matches "@exampleFilter"</div>
                                                    }
                                                    else
                                                    {
                                                        <Virtualize Items="filteredExamples" Context="row" ItemSize="ExampleRowHeight" OverscanCount="6">
                                                            @if (row.IsHeading)
                                                            {
                                                                <div class="examplePickerHeading">@row.Name</div>
                                                            }
                                                            else
                                                            {
                                                                <div class="examplePickerOption @(row.Name == selectedExample ? "isChosen" : "")"
                                                                     role="option"
                                                                     aria-selected="@((row.Name == selectedExample).ToString().ToLowerInvariant())"
                                                                     data-file="@row.Name"
                                                                     title="@row.Name"
                                                                     @onmouseenter="() => previewOnHover(row.Name)"
                                                                     @onclick="() => openExample(row.Name)">@row.Name</div>
                                                            }
                                                        </Virtualize>
                                                    }
                                                </div>
                                            </div>

                                            @*
                                                What was chosen, drawn, beside the list that chose it. The popup stays
                                                open while cells are looked through, so this is the difference between
                                                reading a list of names and seeing what they are.

                                                On selection rather than on hover: an <option> in a native select is
                                                drawn by the operating system and reports nothing back to the page -
                                                no hover, no focus, nothing until it is picked.
                                            *@
                                            @*
                                                A frame that stretches to the height of the list beside it, with the
                                                drawing filling it. The frame is what carries the border and the size,
                                                so the SVG has only to fill its parent - an <svg> told to stretch does
                                                not size the way a block does.
                                            *@
                                            <div class="examplePreviewFrame">
                                                @if (previewMarkup.Length > 0)
                                                {
                                                    <svg class="examplePreview" viewBox="@previewViewBox"
                                                         xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                                                         role="img" aria-label="Preview of @(previewFor.Length > 0 ? previewFor : selectedExample)">
                                                        @((MarkupString)previewMarkup)
                                                    </svg>
                                                }
                                                else
                                                {
                                                    <div class="examplePreviewEmpty">No preview</div>
                                                }
                                            </div>
                                        </div>
                                    </div>

                                    @*Only for a sky130 cell, since that is the only PDK these point at.*@
                                    @if (CurrentlySelectedFileName.Contains("sky130"))
                                    {
                                        <hr class="popupDivider" />

                                        <div class="row">
                                            <p class="mb-0">
                                                <a href="https://skywater-pdk.readthedocs.io/en/main/contents/libraries/foundry-provided.html" target="_blank"
                                                   title="SkyWater's own documentation for the sky130 cell libraries. Opens in a new tab">PDK Docs</a>
                                                <span>, </span>
                                                <a href="https://skywater-pdk.readthedocs.io/en/main/contents/libraries/sky130_fd_sc_hd/cells/nand2/README.html#sky130-fd-sc-hd-nand2" target="_blank"
                                                   title="What one sky130 cell's documentation looks like, using the NAND2 as the example. Opens in a new tab">Example PDK NAND2 Cell Docs</a>
                                            </p>
                                        </div>
                                    }

                                </div>
                            </div>
                         }
                     </div>

                     @*
                         Which view is on, as three boxes with one of them lit.

                         **A switch rather than a menu.** There are exactly three, they never change, and the
                         whole set fits in the room one word takes - so hiding two of them behind a press was
                         charging a click and a wait for something that was already affordable. This is the
                         same shape as the tool group two columns along, which is the other place in the bar
                         where one of a fixed few is chosen, and it says the same thing: these are
                         alternatives, and that one is current.

                         It was a menu before that, and a native select before that. The select was the one
                         control up here drawn by the operating system rather than by the page; the menu
                         fixed that and kept the click nobody needed.

                         `Txt` rather than `Text`, because the three read as a set when they are the same
                         length and the one that is a word among two abbreviations is the odd one.
                     *@
                     <div class="col-1 toolbarItem toolbarView toolbarGroupEnd" style="align-self: end;">
                         @*
                             role=group and aria-pressed, not a radio group: these are buttons that do
                             something when pressed rather than a field with a value, which is what a screen
                             reader is told either way and is the truer of the two here - pressing one
                             switches the screen rather than recording an answer.
                         *@
                         <div id="viewPick" class="toolGroup" role="group" aria-label="View">
                             @foreach ((ViewType which, string says, string shorthand) in Views)
                             {
                                 <button class="buttonStyle2 toolbarButton toolButton buttonIcon viewPickBox @(view == which ? "toolButtonOn" : "")"
                                         data-view="@which"
                                         aria-pressed="@(view == which ? "true" : "false")"
                                         aria-label="@says"
                                         data-shorthand="@shorthand"
                                         title="@says"
                                         @onclick="() => chooseView(which)">
                                     @*
                                         Pictures rather than the tokens, now that the tools two columns
                                         along are pictures too - a row of words next to a row of glyphs
                                         reads as two kinds of control where there is one.

                                         A page of writing, a plan, and a cube: the three drawings every
                                         program uses for exactly these three, and the only trio that tells
                                         2D from 3D without reading them. The words survive as the label a
                                         reader hears and the tooltip a pointer gets.
                                     *@
                                     @if (which == ViewType.ViewText)
                                     {
                                         <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true">
                                             <rect x="2.6" y="1.6" width="10.8" height="12.8" rx="1.4"
                                                   fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" />
                                             <path d="M5.2 5.2h5.6M5.2 8h5.6M5.2 10.8h3.4"
                                                   fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"
                                                   stroke-linecap="round" />
                                         </svg>
                                     }
                                     else if (which == ViewType.View2DSvg)
                                     {
                                         <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true">
                                             <rect x="1.7" y="3.2" width="12.6" height="9.6" rx="1.2"
                                                   fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" />
                                             <path d="M6.6 3.2v9.6M1.7 8.4h4.9"
                                                   fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" />
                                         </svg>
                                     }
                                     else
                                     {
                                         <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true">
                                             <path d="M8 1.5 14.2 5v6L8 14.5 1.8 11V5z"
                                                   fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"
                                                   stroke-linejoin="round" />
                                             <path d="M1.8 5 8 8.5 14.2 5M8 8.5v6"
                                                   fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"
                                                   stroke-linejoin="round" />
                                         </svg>
                                     }
                                 </button>
                             }
                         </div>
                     </div>

                    @*
                        The two sidebars, shown or hidden.

                        Beside the view buttons and not joined to them: those three are one control with a
                        chosen segment - exactly one view is on - and these two are switches that are on or
                        off independently. Butting all five together would say they were the same kind of
                        thing, which is the one claim a joined group makes.

                        In the shell rather than in the 2D view's own stretch of the bar, though the cell
                        tree is drawn by that view. Both lists stand beside the 3D view as well, and a pair
                        of buttons that existed in one view only would be two panels you could hide from one
                        screen and not get back from the other.
                    *@
                    @if (view != ViewType.ViewText && !viewerOnly)
                    {
                        <div class="col-1 toolbarItem toolbarGroupEnd" style="align-self: end;">
                            <div class="toolGroup seamedGroup" role="group" aria-label="Sidebars">
                                @*
                                    Off in the 3D view, because the tree is drawn by the 2D one.

                                    Greyed rather than taken away, which is the choice this bar makes
                                    everywhere: a button that vanishes leaves somebody wondering whether the
                                    app can do the thing at all, where one that is visibly off says the view
                                    they are in has decided. It is also the pair beside it - a switch that
                                    disappeared from one of two would leave a single button where there were
                                    two, and it would not be obvious which one had gone.
                                *@
                                <button id="cellTreeButton" class="buttonStyle2 toolbarButton toolButton buttonIcon @(cellTreeOpen ? "toolButtonOn" : "")"
                                        @onclick="toggleCellTree"
                                        disabled="@(view != ViewType.View2DSvg)"
                                        aria-pressed="@(cellTreeOpen ? "true" : "false")"
                                        aria-label="Cells"
                                        title="@(view != ViewType.View2DSvg ? "The cell tree is drawn beside the 2D view" : cellTreeOpen ? "Hide the cell tree" : "Show the file's cells as a tree, down the side of the view")">
                                    @*
                                        A trunk down the left, three branches of one length, a node on each.

                                        **The nodes are what makes it a tree rather than a bracket.** Four
                                        attempts before this were lines alone - a trunk with stubs coming off
                                        it - and a line meeting a line at a right angle is a bracket, an E, or
                                        a panel with a divider in it, which is what each of them was taken
                                        for. A branch that ends in a *thing* is a branch that leads somewhere,
                                        and three of them hanging off one trunk is the shape every file
                                        explorer draws.

                                        **One length for all three**, which is the correction that prompted
                                        this: they were a long one and two short, so the eye read a heading
                                        with items under it rather than three siblings.

                                        The trunk starts below the top of the box and turns into the bottom
                                        branch at a real corner, so the whole of the left side is one stroke.
                                        A tree's last child has nothing below it, and the turn is what says
                                        so - drawn as a separate stub it read as a mark that had come adrift.
                                    *@
                                    @*
                                        Three nodes on centers 3.2, 8 and 12.8; three branches at those same
                                        three heights, each from the trunk at x 3 to the nodes at x 7.6. The
                                        numbers are written out because they have to agree - a branch half a
                                        unit off its node is a line that stops just short of the box, which
                                        at this size is the difference between joined and nearly joined.
                                    *@
                                    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true">
                                        <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"
                                              stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                              d="M3 1.2V11.4a1.4 1.4 0 0 0 1.4 1.4H7.6M3 3.2H7.6M3 8H7.6" />

                                        @*
                                            Rounded, and filled rather than outlined at this size: a 7.4 by
                                            3.6 box with a 1.5 stroke leaves about half a pixel of hole in the
                                            middle of it, which paints as a smudge rather than as an outline.
                                        *@
                                        <rect x="7.6" y="1.4" width="7.4" height="3.6" rx="1.3" fill="currentColor" />
                                        <rect x="7.6" y="6.2" width="7.4" height="3.6" rx="1.3" fill="currentColor" />
                                        <rect x="7.6" y="11" width="7.4" height="3.6" rx="1.3" fill="currentColor" />
                                    </svg>
                                </button>

                                <button id="layersToggle" class="buttonStyle2 toolbarButton toolButton buttonIcon @(layersOpen ? "toolButtonOn" : "")"
                                        @onclick="toggleLayers"
                                        aria-pressed="@(layersOpen ? "true" : "false")"
                                        aria-label="Layers"
                                        title="@(layersOpen ? "Hide the layer list" : "Show the layer list")">
                                    @*A stack of sheets, which is what a layer list is a list of.*@
                                    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true">
                                        <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"
                                              stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                              d="M8 1.6 1.8 4.8 8 8l6.2-3.2zM1.8 8.4 8 11.6l6.2-3.2M1.8 11.8 8 15l6.2-3.2" />
                                    </svg>
                                </button>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    }


                     @viewer?.Toolbar

                     @*
                         The view, with the page's margins taken away.

                         Last in the bar and pushed to the far end, because it is about the window rather
                         than about the file - everything to its left changes what is drawn or what is being
                         done to it, and this changes how much room there is to do it in.

                         Not the browser's own fullscreen. That hides the tab strip and the address bar,
                         which is the browser's business and needs a user gesture it can refuse; this is the
                         four rems of padding the page puts around itself, which is what is actually in the
                         way. The top bar stays either way, since it is the thing being kept.

                         On the line with everything else, at the far end of it. It was up in the corner,
                         which read as deliberate while it was a white button among white ones and read as
                         stranded once the rest of the bar became one row of squares of one size. Apart from
                         them by where it sits, not by how high it is.
                     *@
                     <div class="col-1 toolbarItem toolbarEnd">
                         @*
                             The title bar, shown or hidden.

                             Beside full screen because it is the same kind of decision - how much of the
                             window the drawing gets - and the smaller of the two: the banner is one band at
                             the top, where full screen is every margin the page has.

                             The layout owns the banner and this page owns the bar, so the press goes up
                             through a cascade; see MainLayout.ToggleBanner. Left out entirely when the
                             address has already said no banner, because a switch that cannot turn anything
                             on is a switch that does nothing.

                             That last part was a comment and not a condition: this asked BannerShowing,
                             which is also false while somebody has merely put the banner away - so it could
                             not be the test, and an embed on banner=false got the button anyway. See
                             MainLayout.BannerCanShow, which is the question this needed.
                         *@
                         @if (Shell is not null && Shell.BannerCanShow && !viewerOnly)
                         {
                             <button id="bannerToggle" class="iconButton toolbarEndButton buttonIcon"
                                     @onclick="() => Shell.ToggleBanner()"
                                     aria-pressed="@(Shell.BannerShowing ? "true" : "false")"
                                     aria-label="Title bar"
                                     title="@(Shell.BannerShowing ? "Hide the title bar" : "Show the title bar")">
                                 @*
                                     A band across the top of a page, which is what the banner is - filled
                                     when it is showing and an outline when it is not, so the button says
                                     which state it is in rather than which one it would take you to.
                                 *@
                                 <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true">
                                     <rect x="1.6" y="2.4" width="12.8" height="11.2" rx="1.4"
                                           fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" />
                                     <path d="M1.6 6.2h12.8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" />
                                     @if (Shell.BannerShowing)
                                     {
                                         <rect x="1.6" y="2.4" width="12.8" height="3.8" rx="1.4" fill="currentColor" />
                                     }
                                 </svg>
                             </button>
                         }

                         <button id="fullScreen" class="iconButton toolbarEndButton buttonIcon" @onclick="toggleFullScreen"
                                 aria-label="@(fullScreen ? "Leave full screen" : "Full screen")"
                                 title="@(fullScreen ? "Leave full screen" : "Full screen the editor")">
                             <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true">
                                 @if (fullScreen)
                                 {
                                     <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                           d="M6.5 1.5v5h-5M9.5 14.5v-5h5" />
                                 }
                                 else
                                 {
                                     <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                           d="M1.5 6V1.5H6M10 1.5h4.5V6M14.5 10v4.5H10M6 14.5H1.5V10" />
                                 }
                             </svg>
                         </button>
                     </div>

                 </div>
             </div>
            }

            @*
                What went wrong with a layermap the address asked for.

                Only ever shown when one was named and could not be used, which for an ordinary visit is
                never. Dismissable, like the download notice: it is about something that has finished, and
                the visitor cannot fix it - see fetchLayerMap for why it is here rather than in an alert.
            *@
            @if (layerMapNotice.Length > 0)
            {
                <div id="layerMapNotice" class="row inlineNotice">
                    <div class="col">@layerMapNotice</div>
                    <div class="col-auto">
                        <button class="buttonStyle2 closeButton" @onclick="dismissLayerMapNotice" title="Dismiss">&times;</button>
                    </div>
                </div>
            }

            @*
                What the file turned out not to be.

                **Three things the flattener has always worked out and nobody was ever told.** Only the CLI
                read them; the app opened a truncated layout, a layout with a reference loop in it, or one
                missing the cells it points at, and drew what it had without a word. In a tool for checking
                layouts, geometry quietly absent is the worst thing that can happen.

                Not dismissable, unlike the download notice. That one is about something that has finished;
                this is about what is on screen right now, and it stops being true only when another file
                is opened.
            *@
            @if (layoutNotice().Length > 0)
            {
                <div id="layoutNotice" class="row inlineNotice">
                    <div class="col">@layoutNotice()</div>
                </div>
            }

            @*The two file lists used to be here, as dialogs. They hang off their own buttons in the bar now.*@

            <!-- ***************************************************************************************************************** -->


            <!-- *************************************************** View ******************************************************** -->

            <div id="viewContainer" class="row">
    
                <!-- ************************************************** View ********************************************************* -->

                @*height: inherit is gone - the row is a flex line now, so this stretches to it on its own.*@
                <div class="col viewBorderBackground viewPane" style="background-color: white; padding: 0; width: 80%;">
                            @switch (view)
                            {
                                case ViewType.ViewText: <TextEditor @ref="viewer" Gds="gds" OnFileChanged="onFileEdited"></TextEditor>;
                                    break;
                                case ViewType.View2DSvg: <Viewer2DSvg @ref="viewer" Gds="gds" Prepared="preparedLayout" ShowLayers="checkboxItems" Markers="@drcMarkers" Notice="@drcNotice" NoticeIsFault="@drcNoticeIsFault" OnNoticeDismissed="dismissDrcNotice" History="editHistory" ReadOnly="noEditing" CellTreeOpen="cellTreeOpen" OnCellTreeClosed="toggleCellTree" OnSettingsChanged="saveSession" OnToolChanged="redrawToolbar" OnFileChanged="onFileEdited" OnLayerRenamed="renamed => applyLayerName(renamed.Key, renamed.Name)"></Viewer2DSvg>;
                                    break;
                                case ViewType.View3D: <Viewer3D @ref="viewer" Gds="gds" ShowLayers="checkboxItems" FileName="@CurrentlySelectedFileName" OnSettingsChanged="saveSession" OnToolChanged="redrawToolbar"></Viewer3D>;
                                    break;

                                default:
                                    break;
                            }

                    @*
                        Centering, over the drawing rather than up in the bar.

                        It belongs to what is on screen, not to the page around it - the same reading that
                        puts Download Image on the canvas rather than beside the format picker. Full screen
                        and the banner switch stayed in the bar because those two really are about the page.

                        Here rather than inside either view, because both of them answer it and neither of
                        them owns the question: it is one button and one handler, and the pane is the box
                        both views are rendered into. Which also means it survives a view switch without
                        being written twice.

                        Top right, so it is the corner nothing else uses. The selection panel is top left,
                        Download Image is bottom right and the 3D view's Admire button is too - see
                        .viewAction, whose look this shares and whose corner it does not.

                        2D and 3D only. The text view is a list of records with no framing to put back, and
                        a button that did nothing on a third of the views would be worse than one that is
                        not there - see centerView for the same reasoning in C#.
                    *@
                    @*
                        What the last download could not carry, over the drawing rather than above it.

                        **The same box the drawing hint uses**, because it is the same kind of statement: a
                        line about what just happened, that appears and goes away. As a bar across the page
                        it pushed the whole view down to say something most files never need said, and the
                        layout jumped under the pointer at the moment somebody was reading about it.

                        Here in the pane rather than inside a view, for the reason the Center button below
                        gives: a download happens from the toolbar in every view, so a notice living in the
                        2D component would be a notice the 3D and text views could not show.

                        Above the view's own hint if they ever coincide. This is news and that is
                        reference - and only one of the two can be up at a time in there anyway, since the
                        drawing hint and the rule result share one chain.
                    *@
                    @if (downloadNotice.Length > 0)
                    {
                        <div id="downloadNotice" class="drawHint viewNotice">
                            <span>@downloadNotice</span>

                            <button class="viewNoticeClose" @onclick="dismissDownloadNotice"
                                    aria-label="Dismiss this notice" title="Dismiss">&times;</button>
                        </div>
                    }

                    @if (view == ViewType.View2DSvg || view == ViewType.View3D)
                    {
                        <button id="centerView" class="iconButton viewAction viewActionTop" @onclick="centerView"
                                aria-label="Center the view"
                                title="Put the layout back in the middle of the window">
                            @*
                                Four arrows closing on a point, which is the thing this does. The crosshair
                                that was here first reads as *aiming* at something rather than gathering it
                                back, and at sixteen pixels its ticks were thin enough to be mistaken for
                                the full-screen corners two buttons along.
                            *@
                            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true">
                                <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="1.7" fill="currentColor" />
                                <path fill="currentColor" d="M5.9 1.3h4.2L8 4.4z" />
                                <path fill="currentColor" d="M5.9 14.7h4.2L8 11.6z" />
                                <path fill="currentColor" d="M1.3 5.9v4.2L4.4 8z" />
                                <path fill="currentColor" d="M14.7 5.9v4.2L11.6 8z" />
                            </svg>
                        </button>
                    }
                        </div>
    
                <!-- ***************************************************************************************************************** -->
    
                <!-- ************************************************** Layers ******************************************************* -->
                
                @*And the layer list goes with the bar, for the same reason: the canvas on its own.*@
                @if (view != ViewType.ViewText && !viewerOnly && layersOpen)
                {
                    <div id="layerSidebar" class="col-1 viewBorderBackground">

                        @*
                            The edge, to drag the list wider or narrower - see sidebars.js.

                            On the left, which is this panel's inner edge and the one a pointer reaches from
                            the drawing. Which is also why it grows the other way from the cell tree's: the
                            pointer moving right makes a left-hand panel wider and this one narrower.
                        *@
                        <div class="sidebarGrabber sidebarGrabberLeft"
                             data-sidebar-grab="layerSidebar"
                             data-sidebar-variable="--layer-sidebar"
                             data-sidebar-towards="left"
                             role="separator" aria-orientation="vertical"
                             title="Drag to resize"></div>


                        @*
                            The heading, and the way out of it.

                            A cross as well as the button in the bar, which is the pair the cell tree has:
                            the button is how it is got back, and this is where a hand already is when the
                            list is the thing in the way.
                        *@
                        <div class="sidebarTitle">
                            @*
                                Both names, and the one you are on is lit.

                                **A switch that shows only the current state cannot say what it would do.**
                                A single heading reading "Layers" had to carry arrows and a tooltip to hint
                                that a second list existed at all, and somebody who never hovered it never
                                found out. Two words side by side say there are two lists in the first
                                glance, and pressing the unlit one is an obvious thing to try.

                                Where a toolbar button for the rules used to be, and better than one: this
                                sits on the panel it changes rather than in a bar across the page.

                                Only where the rules can be checked. Outside the 2D view there is nothing to
                                switch to, so it goes back to being a plain heading rather than a control
                                that does nothing.
                            *@
                            @if (view == ViewType.View2DSvg)
                            {
                                <div class="sidebarTitleMiddle">
                                    <div class="sidebarPanelPick" role="group" aria-label="Which list this panel shows">
                                        <button id="sidebarPanelLayers"
                                                class="sidebarPanelChoice @(sidebarShows == SidebarPanel.Layers ? "sidebarPanelChoiceOn" : "")"
                                                @onclick="() => showPanel(SidebarPanel.Layers)"
                                                aria-pressed="@(sidebarShows == SidebarPanel.Layers ? "true" : "false")"
                                                title="What is drawn, and how">Layers</button>

                                        <button id="sidebarPanelRules"
                                                class="sidebarPanelChoice @(sidebarShows == SidebarPanel.Rules ? "sidebarPanelChoiceOn" : "")"
                                                @onclick="() => showPanel(SidebarPanel.Rules)"
                                                aria-pressed="@(sidebarShows == SidebarPanel.Rules ? "true" : "false")"
                                                title="What it is allowed to be, and whether it is">Rules</button>
                                    </div>

                                    @*
                                        What this panel is for, since neither answer is guessable.

                                        **A layermap and a rule deck are both files the user has to supply**, and
                                        nothing on screen says why - a viewer that simply drew the file would need
                                        neither. The explanation is a few paragraphs, which is too much for a
                                        tooltip and not enough for a page, so it is a panel you open and a guide
                                        you can take away.

                                        **Opened by clicking, not by hovering.** A box that size arriving over the
                                        middle of the screen because the pointer crossed a corner of the sidebar is
                                        something you have to get rid of rather than something you asked for. What
                                        hovering does is say the icon is worth clicking, which is all a hover
                                        should have to carry.
                                    *@
                                    <button id="sidebarInfo" class="sidebarInfoButton @(infoOpen ? "sidebarInfoButtonOn" : "")"
                                            @onclick="toggleInfo"
                                            @onmouseenter="showInfoHint" @onmouseleave="hideInfoHint"
                                            aria-label="@(sidebarShows == SidebarPanel.Rules ? "About design rules" : "About layers")">
                                        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="13" height="13" aria-hidden="true">
                                            <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="6.6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" />
                                            <circle cx="8" cy="4.6" r="0.95" fill="currentColor" />
                                            <path d="M8 7.1v4.6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" />
                                        </svg>
                                    </button>

                                    @*
                                        The nudge, under the icon rather than over the drawing.

                                        Small on purpose: it says what pressing does and nothing else, because the
                                        thing it is describing is one click away and will say the rest itself.
                                        Not shown while the full panel is up - it would be describing a button
                                        whose result is already on screen.
                                    *@
                                    @if (infoHintShown && !infoOpen)
                                    {
                                        <div id="sidebarInfoHint" class="sidebarInfoHint" role="tooltip">
                                            Click for what @(sidebarShows == SidebarPanel.Rules ? "design rules" : "layers")
                                            are, and how to make the file this panel takes
                                        </div>
                                    }
                                </div>
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                <b>Layers</b>
                            }

                            @*
                                Named after whichever list is up, since the panel is now two of them.

                                **And never with "panel" in it.** A role-and-name lookup matches the name as
                                a substring, so "Close the panel" answers to a search for "Pan" - which is
                                the toolbar's pan tool, and three ruler tests started resolving to two
                                elements the moment this button was renamed. Nothing about the app was
                                wrong; the word simply contains the other one.
                            *@
                            <button id="layersClose" class="cellListClose"
                                    @onclick="toggleLayers"
                                    aria-label="@(sidebarShows == SidebarPanel.Rules ? "Close the rules" : "Close the layer list")"
                                    title="Close">&#128473;</button>
                        </div>

                        @*
                            What the panel is, over the middle of the screen.

                            Centered rather than hung off the icon because it is a few paragraphs rather than a
                            tooltip, and a box that size anchored to a corner control ends up half off the window
                            on the narrow layouts this app is used at.

                            It closes when the pointer leaves it, so moving away is enough and nothing has to be
                            dismissed. The cross is for the pinned case, where a click opened it.
                        *@
                        @if (infoOpen)
                        {
                            <div id="sidebarInfoPopup" class="popupDiv infoPopup" @onmouseleave="closeInfo">
                                <div class="infoPopupHead">
                                    <b>@(sidebarShows == SidebarPanel.Rules ? "Design rules" : "Layers")</b>

                                    <button id="closeSidebarInfo" class="buttonStyle2 closeButton" @onclick="closeInfo"
                                            aria-label="Close" title="Close">&times;</button>
                                </div>

                                @if (sidebarShows == SidebarPanel.Rules)
                                {
                                    <p>
                                        A <b>design rule</b> is the foundry's answer to "how thin may this wire be,
                                        and how close may two of them get". A deck of them, checked against your
                                        layout, is what catches a shape that could not be manufactured.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        <b>There is no standard file to download.</b> Design rules have no
                                        interchange format: a foundry supporting three tools ships three separately
                                        maintained decks that are meant to agree, and nothing converts between them.
                                        KLayout's is a Ruby program, Magic's is fused into its technology file, and
                                        Calibre's SVRF is proprietary and usually under NDA. So a deck here is a
                                        small text file you supply — the same arrangement as the layermap, and for
                                        the same licensing reason: one PDK's tables are somebody else's work and do
                                        not belong compiled into a viewer that opens any file.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        The format is deliberately a fixed vocabulary rather than an expression
                                        language. <b>A rule this build cannot measure is refused by name</b> —
                                        listed, and every result from that deck says "not fully checked" — because
                                        a count of faults is only an answer when every rule actually ran.
                                    </p>

                                    <p class="infoPopupWarn">
                                        This is not a signoff tool. It catches the obvious against the rules you
                                        give it; it is not a tapeout.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        <b>Load sky130A example</b> in the panel loads a working 30-rule deck to
                                        read. To write one for your own PDK, take the guide below: it is the whole
                                        grammar, written so you can hand it to an AI along with your PDK's rule
                                        document and get a deck back.
                                    </p>
                                    <p>
                                        <b>Nothing failing?</b> That is the expected answer — the bundled cells
                                        are signed-off sky130 layout, so a correct deck finds nothing in them.
                                        To see what a violation actually looks like, load a deck whose limits
                                        the layout cannot meet:
                                    </p>

                                    <div class="infoPopupActions">
                                        <button id="downloadDeckGuide" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton"
                                                @onclick='() => downloadGuide("WRITING-A-DECK.md")'>
                                            Download the deck-writing guide (.md)
                                        </button>

                                        <button id="loadStrictDeck" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton infoPopupDemo"
                                                @onclick="loadStrictDeck">
                                            Load a deck that fails (demo)
                                        </button>
                                    </div>
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <p>
                                        A GDSII file carries only numbers. <b>Nothing in the format records that
                                        65/20 is diffusion</b>, what color it should be drawn in, what it is for, or
                                        where it sits in the wafer.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        So all of that comes from a <b>layermap</b> you supply — a CSV of
                                        layer, datatype, name, color, height, thickness and role. Without one the
                                        app draws bare numbers in palette colors, which is readable but says
                                        nothing about the process.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        The <b>role</b> column is the one worth filling in: net tracing follows
                                        conductors through vias, so a via with no role stops a net dead, and antenna
                                        rules are refused outright rather than guessed at without it.
                                    </p>

                                    <p>
                                        <b>Export</b> is the fastest start — it writes every layer pair already in
                                        your file, so filling one in means typing over rather than typing out. To
                                        build one for a whole PDK, take the guide below and hand it to an AI with
                                        your PDK's layer table.
                                    </p>

                                    <button id="downloadLayermapGuide" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton"
                                            @onclick='() => downloadGuide("WRITING-A-LAYERMAP.md")'>
                                        Download the layermap-writing guide (.md)
                                    </button>
                                }
                            </div>
                        }

                        @*
                            Naming lives beside the list it renames rather than in the top toolbar, which is
                            about the file as a whole. A GDSII file carries only numbers - what 65/20 means is
                            PDK data - so the mapping is loaded the way KLayout loads a .lyp or Magic reads its
                            techfile, from a file the user picks.
                        *@
                        @*
                            Each control says what it does with the icon the toolbar already uses for it -
                            up for the file going in, down for the one coming out - so the pair reads as
                            load and save rather than as a label and a link.
                        *@
                        @if (sidebarShows == SidebarPanel.Rules)
                        {
                            @*
                                The rules, where the layers usually are.

                                **The same panel showing a different thing**, rather than a second panel: both
                                are a list down the side of the drawing with a file loaded into it, and they are
                                never both wanted at once - one says what is drawn and the other what it is
                                allowed to be.

                                Import and Export keep their places and their meanings. What they carry is the
                                deck rather than the layermap, which is the one thing that changes with the
                                panel.
                            *@
                            <div class="layerSidebarControls">
                                <InputFile id="drcDeckImport" style="display: none;" OnChange="onDrcDeckChosen" />
                                <label for="drcDeckImport" class="layerSidebarAction" title="Load a design rule deck - see docs/DRC.md for the format">
                                    <img src="./resources/Images/Icons/upload.png" alt="" /> Import
                                </label>

                                @if (drcDeck is not null)
                                {
                                    <span id="drcExport" class="layerSidebarAction" @onclick="exportDeck"
                                          title="Write the deck in force back out as a file">
                                        <img src="./resources/Images/Icons/download.png" alt="" /> Export
                                    </span>
                                }

                                @if (drcDeck is not null)
                                {
                                    @*
                                        Drops the deck, the way Clear drops a layermap one panel over.

                                        The two sit in the same place in the same row of the same panel, so
                                        they had better mean the same thing. This one used to keep the deck
                                        and take only the markers off, which made one Clear a reset and the
                                        other a tidy-up - a difference nothing on screen explained.
                                    *@
                                    <span id="drcClear" class="layerSidebarAction layerSidebarClear" @onclick="clearDrc"
                                          title="Drop the deck and everything it found, leaving the panel empty">Clear</span>
                                }

                                @*
                                    The bundled deck, offered only while there is nothing loaded.

                                    **Hover to open, click the button inside to load.** Two steps rather than
                                    one because this replaces what is in the panel, and a control that swaps
                                    your work for a sample on a single mis-click is a control people learn to
                                    steer around. The popup is inside the wrapper, so moving onto it never
                                    crosses a gap and it needs no state to stay open.
                                *@
                                @if (drcDeck is null)
                                {
                                    <span class="exampleOffer">
                                        <span class="layerSidebarAction" id="drcExampleOffer">Example</span>

                                        <span class="exampleOfferPopup">
                                            <button id="drcBundled" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton" @onclick="loadBundledDeck"
                                                    title="The 30-rule sky130 starter deck that ships with the examples">Load sky130</button>
                                        </span>
                                    </span>
                                }
                            </div>

                            @*
                                Check, and whether to keep checking.

                                **The switch is beside the button rather than in the settings**, because the
                                two are one question - when the layout gets measured - and reading the answer
                                should not mean opening a menu to find half of it.

                                **The button stays live with the switch on.** It went disabled, on the
                                argument that a control with nothing left to do should say so. But there is
                                always something left to do: a check runs on an *edit*, and plenty happens
                                that is not one - a deck imported, a cell flattened, or simply wanting the
                                marks back after reading them away. Taking the only manual run away because
                                an automatic one exists left no way to ask for the thing the panel is for.
                            *@
                            @if (drcDeck is not null)
                            {
                                <div class="rulesRun">
                                    <button id="drcRun" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton" @onclick="runDrc"
                                            title="@drcDeckDescription()">DRC Check</button>

                                    <label class="rulesContinuous" title="@drcContinuousDescription()">
                                        <input type="checkbox" id="drcContinuous" checked="@drcContinuous" @onchange="toggleContinuous" />
                                        check on edit
                                    </label>
                                </div>
                            }

                            @*
                                The rules themselves, listed where the layers are listed.

                                Numbered like the layer rows, so the two panels read as the same shape. What each
                                row says is the rule's own id, what it measures and the limit it measures
                                against - which is what a deck is, one line at a time.
                            *@
                            <div class="layerList rulesList">
                                @if (drcDeck is null)
                                {
                                    @*
                                        Nothing loaded, and no offer here any more.

                                        **The offer moved up to the controls row, as Example.** It was here,
                                        under a sentence explaining it, which was two things saying one -
                                        and it left the layer panel with no equivalent at all. One control
                                        in the same place in both panels is the shorter answer.
                                    *@
                                    <div class="rulesEmpty">
                                        No rules loaded. <b>Import</b> a deck of your own, or take the bundled
                                        sky130 one from <b>Example</b> above.
                                    </div>
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    @for (int i = 0; i < drcDeck.Rules.Count; i++)
                                    {
                                        var rule = drcDeck.Rules[i];

                                        @*
                                            A broken rule's row goes to the first fault under it.

                                            Which is what a violation carrying its bounds is for, and it is the
                                            one piece of the old results list worth keeping here: the markers
                                            say where every fault is, and this says where to start looking.
                                        *@
                                        var broken = brokenRules.Contains(rule.Id);
                                        var id = rule.Id;

                                        <div class="rulesRow @(broken ? "rulesRowBroken" : "")"
                                             title="@rule.Description"
                                             @onclick="() => showRule(id)">
                                            <span class="rulesNumber">@(i + 1).</span>
                                            <span class="rulesId">@rule.Id</span>
                                            <span class="rulesCheckName">@rule.Check.ToString().ToLowerInvariant()</span>
                                            <span class="rulesLimit">@rule.Value</span>
                                            @if (broken)
                                            {
                                                <span class="rulesCount">@countFor(id)</span>
                                            }
                                        </div>
                                    }

                                    @*
                                        A rule the deck holds and this build cannot measure is listed too, and
                                        listed as refused. Leaving it out would make the panel say the deck is
                                        smaller than it is.
                                    *@
                                    @foreach (string refused in drcDeck.Refused)
                                    {
                                        <div class="rulesRow rulesRowRefused" title="@refused">
                                            <span class="rulesId">@refused.Split(':')[0]</span>
                                            <span class="rulesRefusedWord">not measurable</span>
                                        </div>
                                    }
                                }
                            </div>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <div class="layerSidebarControls">
                                @*
                                    Import and Export rather than Names and Template.

                                    The pair carries more than names now - a color, a height and a thickness -
                                    so "Names" undersold what loading one does and "Template" named a starting
                                    point rather than what the button actually writes, which is this file's
                                    layers as they currently stand.
                                *@
                                <InputFile id="layerNamesImport" style="display: none;" OnChange="onLayerNamesChosen" />
                                <label for="layerNamesImport" class="layerSidebarAction" title="Load layers from a CSV of layer,datatype,name - and optionally a color, a height and a thickness">
                                    <img src="./resources/Images/Icons/upload.png" alt="" /> Import
                                </label>

                                @if (gds is not null)
                                {
                                    <span id="layerNamesExport" class="layerSidebarAction" @onclick="exportLayerNames" title="Write this file's layers out as a CSV: their names, colors, heights and thicknesses">
                                        <img src="./resources/Images/Icons/download.png" alt="" /> Export
                                    </span>
                                }

                                @if (layerNamesLoaded)
                                {
                                    @*
                                        Framed, unlike the two beside it.

                                        Import and Export each carry a mark, and a word with a picture beside it
                                        reads as something you press. This one is a bare word in a row of them,
                                        and the only thing saying it is a control was the cursor - which you have
                                        to already be over it to find out. A border is the cheapest way to say
                                        so; no fill, because it is the most destructive of the three and should
                                        not be the loudest.
                                    *@
                                    <span class="layerSidebarAction layerSidebarClear" @onclick="clearLayerNames" title="Drop every layer name and put the palette colors back, leaving the bare numbers">Clear</span>
                                }

                                @*
                                    The bundled layermap, on the same terms as the deck one panel over.

                                    Offered only while nothing is named, and behind a hover so a mis-click
                                    cannot replace a mapping somebody typed. On a bundled example it has
                                    usually already been applied and this never appears; it is here for an
                                    uploaded sky130 file, which gets no mapping guessed at it.
                                *@
                                @if (!layerNamesLoaded)
                                {
                                    <span class="exampleOffer">
                                        <span class="layerSidebarAction" id="layerExampleOffer">Example</span>

                                        <span class="exampleOfferPopup">
                                            <button id="layerNamesBundled" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton" @onclick="loadBundledLayerNames"
                                                    title="The sky130 layer names, colors, heights and roles that ship with the examples">Load sky130</button>
                                        </span>
                                    </span>
                                }
                            </div>

                            @*
                                Labels used to have one switch here, for the whole file. They are per layer now,
                                in each row's settings - which labels are worth reading depends on the layer, and
                                one switch could only ever be all of them or none.
                            *@

                            @*
                                A grid rather than bootstrap rows of fit-content columns, which is what left the
                                list ragged: every cell sized itself to its own contents, so the numbers, the
                                swatches and the checkboxes each sat at a different place on every line. Fixed
                                tracks line all four up, and the grid as a whole is centered in the sidebar.
                            *@
                            <div class="layerList">
                                @if(view != ViewType.ViewText)
                                {
                                    @for (int i = 0; i < checkboxItems.Count; i++)
                                    {
                                            var item = checkboxItems[i];

                                            @*
                                                The row is a control while the Draw tool is out: clicking it
                                                takes that layer as the one a shape goes on, and the row is
                                                marked so the answer is where the layers are rather than only
                                                in a dropdown at the top of the screen. The rest of the time
                                                it is the list it always was.
                                            *@
                                            @*
                                                The right button offers what can be done to the layer: name it,
                                                and copy or delete the shapes it holds in the cell being edited.

                                                **Drawn by the 2D view rather than here**, because two of the
                                                three are edits to the layout and this list is only a list -
                                                everything that acts on shapes lives in that component, and a
                                                second menu here would be a second set of the same three
                                                answers to keep in step. See Viewer2DSvg.OpenLayerMenu. In the
                                                3D and text views there is no such component and no menu, which
                                                is right: neither of them can act on a shape.
                                            *@
                                            <div class="layerRow @(drawingView is not null ? "layerRowPickable" : "") @(drawingOnLayer(item) ? "layerRowDrawing" : "")"
                                                 @onclick="() => useDrawLayer(item)"
                                                 @oncontextmenu="e => openLayerRowMenu(item, e)"
                                                 @oncontextmenu:preventDefault="true"
                                                 title="@rowTitle(item)">
                                                <span class="layerIndex">@(i + 1).</span>

                                                @*
                                                    The layer's color, where it is of use: next to the layer.
                                                    It only reports - the gear at the end of the row is what
                                                    opens the settings. A swatch that was itself a button meant
                                                    the row had two things to press and no way to tell which
                                                    did what, and the one that looked most like a control was
                                                    the one that was really a readout.
                                                *@
                                                @*
                                                    A pattern shows here too, or the list stops describing the
                                                    drawing: two layers a shade apart are told apart in the view
                                                    by their hatch and would be two identical swatches in the
                                                    one place that exists to say which layer is which.

                                                    An SVG only when there is one, so the ordinary case is the
                                                    span it always was rather than eight elements per row.
                                                *@
                                                @if (fillOf(item) == LayerFill.None)
                                                {
                                                    <span class="layerSwatch"
                                                          style="background-color: @colorOf(item.Id);"
                                                          title="@colorOf(item.Id)"></span>
                                                }
                                                else
                                                {
                                                    <svg class="layerSwatch layerSwatchFilled" viewBox="0 0 16 16">
                                                        @*
                                                            A child element, not a title attribute: an inline
                                                            SVG is not an HTML element, and the attribute that
                                                            gives every span in this row its tooltip does
                                                            nothing here. The span above keeps using the
                                                            attribute because it is a span.
                                                        *@
                                                        <title>@colorOf(item.Id), @fillOf(item)</title>

                                                        @((MarkupString)SvgWriter.SwatchFor(fillOf(item), colorOf(item.Id), $"rowFill_{SvgWriter.ClassFor(item.Id)}", 16, patternColorOf(item), patternPixelsOf(item)))
                                                    </svg>
                                                }

                                                @*
                                                    The row turns into a text box while it is the one being
                                                    renamed. Editing in place rather than in a dialog because
                                                    naming a layer is only useful next to the geometry it
                                                    names - you look at the shape to work out what it is.
                                                *@
                                                @if (editingLayer == item.Id)
                                                {
                                                    <input type="text"
                                                           class="layerNameBox"
                                                           value="@editingName"
                                                           @oninput="onLayerNameInput"
                                                           @onkeydown="onLayerNameKey"
                                                           @onblur="commitLayerName"
                                                           @ref="layerNameBox"
                                                           placeholder="@item.Id.ToString()" />
                                                }
                                                else
                                                {
                                                    <span class="layerName"
                                                          @onclick="() => startRenaming(item)"
                                                          title="Click to name this layer">@item.Label</span>
                                                }

                                                @*
                                                    Stopped at the checkbox and at the gear, so a press on
                                                    either is only what it is. Hiding a layer and taking it as
                                                    the one to draw on in the same click are two answers to
                                                    opposite questions.
                                                *@
                                                <input type="checkbox"
                                                       class="layerVisible"
                                                       @onchange="async e => await layersChanged(e, item)"
                                                       @onclick:stopPropagation="true"
                                                       checked="@item.IsSelected"
                                                       title="Draw this layer" />

                                                @*
                                                    Everything about how this one layer is drawn, behind one
                                                    control: its color and whether its labels are shown. Drawn
                                                    as an inline SVG rather than an icon file, so it inherits
                                                    the row's color and stays sharp at any zoom without another
                                                    asset to vendor.
                                                *@
                                                <button type="button"
                                                        class="layerSettingsButton"
                                                        title="Color and labels for this layer"
                                                        @onclick="e => openLayerSettings(item, e)"
                                                        @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
                                                    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="13" height="13" aria-hidden="true">
                                                        <path fill="currentColor" d="M6.8 1h2.4l.3 1.9.9.5 1.7-.9 1.7 1.7-.9 1.7.5.9 1.9.3v2.4l-1.9.3-.5.9.9 1.7-1.7 1.7-1.7-.9-.9.5-.3 1.9H6.8l-.3-1.9-.9-.5-1.7.9-1.7-1.7.9-1.7-.5-.9L.7 9.2V6.8l1.9-.3.5-.9-.9-1.7 1.7-1.7 1.7.9.9-.5L6.8 1z" />
                                                        <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="2.4" fill="#fff" />
                                                    </svg>
                                                </button>
                                            </div>
                                    }
                                }
                            </div>
                        }

                        @*
                            How see-through the shapes are drawn, under whichever list is up.

                            Outside the Layers/Rules switch rather than inside it: opacity is about the drawing,
                            and the drawing is there whichever list somebody is reading. Inside the layer branch it
                            disappeared the moment they pressed Rules - which is exactly when a marker over a stack
                            of half-transparent shapes is hardest to see.

                            It was an overlay across the top of the view, sitting on top of the very thing it
                            dims - a control you had to look past to see its own effect. It belongs with the
                            list of what it is dimming.

                            Only for the 2D view, which is the only one that has an opacity; the 3D view
                            stacks its layers in space instead. Reached by a cast rather than through
                            IRenderable, for the same reason the draw layer is: it is not a question every
                            view can answer.
                        *@
                        @if (viewer is Viewer2DSvg shaded)
                        {
                            <div class="layerOpacity">
                                <label for="layerOpacity">Opacity</label>

                                <input id="layerOpacity" type="range" min="0" max="1" step="0.1"
                                       value="@SvgWriter.FormatOpacity(shaded.Opacity)"
                                       @oninput="shaded.AdjustOpacity"
                                       title="How see-through the shapes are drawn, so you can look through stacked layers" />
                            </div>
                        }
                        else if (viewer is Viewer3D stacked)
                        {
                            @*
                                The 3D view's answer to the same question, in the same place.

                                Opacity and spacing are the two ways of seeing past the layer on top, one per
                                view - so whichever view is open, the control for it is the row under the
                                layer list rather than something floating over the drawing in one view and
                                not the other. It was an overlay across the bottom left of the scene, where
                                it sat over the geometry and took drags the orbit controls wanted.
                            *@
                            <div class="layerSpacing">
                                <label for="layerSpacing">Distance</label>

                                <input id="layerSpacing" type="range" min="50" max="700"
                                       value="@stacked.LayerSpacing"
                                       @oninput="stacked.AdjustLayerSpacing"
                                       title="How far apart the layers are stacked, to pull the layout open and see between them" />
                            </div>
                        }

                    </div>
                }

                <!-- ***************************************************************************************************************** -->
    
            </div>

            <!-- ***************************************************************************************************************** -->
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ************************************************************************************************************** -->

    @*
        What to do with a file opened while another one is already open.

        A modal rather than a bar notice, because nothing should be drawn or thrown away until it is
        answered - the two answers lead to different files being on screen, and a notice that can be ignored
        would leave the app having to pick one anyway.

        What the incoming file holds is on the panel because it is what the answer turns on: a file of one
        cell reads differently from a file of forty, and a name clash or a units mismatch is exactly the kind
        of thing that would otherwise be discovered later, in the drawing, as something looking wrong.
    *@
    @if (importPlan is not null)
    {
        <div id="importBackdrop" class="importBackdrop" @onclick="cancelImport"></div>

        <div id="importDialog" class="popupDiv popupZIndex_0 importDialog" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
            <h5 class="popupTitle">@importName</h5>

            <p class="importSummary">
                @describeImport()
            </p>

            @if (importPlan.Renamed.Count > 0)
            {
                @*
                    Said before it happens rather than discovered afterwards in the cell list. A renamed cell
                    is the file's own name being taken by the layout that was open first, which is nobody's
                    mistake and still surprising to meet in a tree.
                *@
                <p class="importNote">
                    @renamedNote()
                </p>
            }

            @if (importPlan.Scale != 1)
            {
                <p class="importNote">
                    The two files measure in different units, so what comes in is resized to match this one.
                </p>
            }

            <div class="importButtons">
                <button id="importAsCell" class="buttonStyle2 importChoice" @onclick="importAsCell"
                        title="@($"Put its cells in this library and carry {importPlan.TopCell} on the pointer")">
                    Add to this layout
                </button>

                <button id="importAsFile" class="buttonStyle2 importChoice" @onclick="openImportedOnItsOwn"
                        title="Close what is open and show this file instead">
                    Open on its own
                </button>

                <button id="importCancel" class="buttonStyle2 importChoice" @onclick="cancelImport"
                        title="Keep what is open and forget this file">
                    Cancel
                </button>
            </div>
        </div>
    }

    <!-- ************************************************************************************************************** -->

    @*
        One layer's settings, as a popup like the About and QR ones rather than something that grows out of
        the sidebar. In the sidebar it pushed the layer list around as it opened and closed, and it had only
        the sidebar's width to lay a row of history swatches out in.

        It began as a color picker and holds everything about how one layer is drawn now - its color and
        whether its labels are shown.
    *@
    @if (settingsFor is not null)
    {
        @*
            The height it may take is measured from where it opens, not from the top of the window - it is
            fixed at the pointer and grows downwards, so a cap in the stylesheet alone bounds nothing. Without
            this the bottom rows fall off the screen on a short window with no way to reach them, which is
            what a Role row did the day it was added.
        *@
        <div class="popupDiv popupZIndex_0 layerSettingsPopup"
             style="left: @(pickerLeft.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))px; top: @(pickerTop.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))px; max-height: calc(100vh - @(pickerTop.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))px - 12px);">
            @*
                The title is the name box. A layer's name is the one thing about it worth typing, and it
                was only reachable by clicking the row's own text in a sidebar narrow enough that the box
                had no room - here it has the width of the popup.

                It holds what the layer is actually called, which for an unnamed one is its pair. That was
                a placeholder over an empty box, which reads as a field waiting to be filled rather than
                as the name it already has - and left nothing to select and type over.

                Typing the pair back, or emptying the box, is taken as no name rather than as naming a
                layer after itself. See commitSettingsName.
            *@
            @*
                A title, because the panel had none.

                It opens over the layout with a name box, two numbers, a role and a color field in it, and
                nothing saying what any of that belongs to. The row it is opened from is a layer, and once
                the popup is up that row is behind it - so the one word that was missing is what this is.

                Left, with the close button still at the far end, which is what the header was laid out for
                before the name moved down to a line of its own.
            *@
            <div class="layerSettingsHeader">
                <span class="layerSettingsTitle">Layer settings</span>

                <button type="button" class="buttonStyle2 closeButton" @onclick="closeLayerSettings"
                        title="Close. Anything changed here has already been applied">&times;</button>
            </div>

            @*Closes the header off the way the footer closes the body, so the two rules bracket it.*@
            <hr class="layerSettingsRule" />

            @*
                Named like the switch below it, and laid out like it: what this layer is called and whether
                its labels are drawn are the two things here that are not the color, so they read as a pair
                of rows rather than as a field and then a setting.
            *@
            <label class="layerSettingsRow" title="What this layer is called. Clearing it, or typing @settingsFor.Id back, leaves it unnamed">
                <span>Name:</span>
                <input type="text"
                       class="layerSettingsName"
                       value="@settingsName"
                       @oninput="onSettingsNameInput"
                       @onkeydown="onSettingsNameKey"
                       @onblur="commitSettingsName" />
            </label>

            <label class="layerSettingsRow" title="Draw the TEXT elements that sit on this layer">
                <span>Show Labels:</span>
                <input type="checkbox" class="layerLabelsToggle" checked="@settingsFor.ShowLabels" @onchange="labelsToggled" />
            </label>

            @*
                No rule between this and the two boxes below.

                Name, labels, height and thickness are one run of settings about the layer itself, and a
                line through the middle of them said they were two groups. The rules that are left bracket
                that whole run off from the color picker under it.
            *@

            @*
                Where this layer sits in the wafer, and how thick it is.

                The 3D view stacks layers evenly until it is told otherwise, which is a guess that says
                only what order they are in. A process gives each layer a real height and thickness, and
                typing those here is what turns the picture from evenly spaced planes into the shape of an
                actual stack - the same thing GDS3D takes a process definition file for.

                Both in database units, which for a file whose UNITS say a database unit is a nanometer -
                as every bundled example does - means a process table can be typed in as it stands.
            *@
            <label class="layerSettingsRow" title="How far up the stack this layer sits, in database units. Blank puts it back on the even spacing">
                <span>Height:</span>
                <input type="number" step="1"
                       class="layerSettingsStack layerSettingsHeight"
                       value="@stackValue(settingsFor, height: true)"
                       @onchange="e => stackTyped(e, height: true)" />
            </label>

            <label class="layerSettingsRow" title="How thick this layer is drawn, in database units">
                <span>Thickness:</span>
                <input type="number" step="1" min="1"
                       class="layerSettingsStack layerSettingsThickness"
                       value="@stackValue(settingsFor, height: false)"
                       @onchange="e => stackTyped(e, height: false)" />
            </label>

            @*
                What the layer is for, which is the one thing here that no PDK table already carries and the
                one thing tracing a net needs. A file says which numbers it uses and never which of them are
                metal, so somebody has to say - and this is where, since the alternative is editing a CSV.
            *@
            <label class="layerSettingsRow" title="Whether this layer carries a net along itself, joins the layers it overlaps, or takes no part. Needed to trace a net">
                <span>Role:</span>
                <select class="layerSettingsRole" value="@roleOf(settingsFor)" @onchange="roleChosen">
                    <option value="@LayerRole.None">none</option>
                    <option value="@LayerRole.Conductor">conductor</option>
                    <option value="@LayerRole.Via">via</option>
                </select>
            </label>

            <hr class="layerSettingsRule" />

            @*
                What is drawn over the color, which is why it sits with the color rather than with the stack.

                **Because color runs out before layers do.** A palette is the hue wheel divided by however
                many layers a file has, and past a dozen the steps are smaller than the difference an
                overlapping stack of half-transparent shapes makes to any of them. A pattern is a second axis
                - the same green, hatched rather than solid - and it survives being printed in gray.

                As buttons rather than a dropdown, because these are pictures. A list of the words "dots",
                "grid", "diagonal" makes somebody read a name and imagine the result; eight tiles make it the
                thing it is. They are the real patterns too, drawn by the same code that fills the layer -
                see patternTile - so a swatch cannot come to disagree with what the choice does.
            *@
            <div class="layerSettingsRow layerSettingsFills" title="A pattern over this layer's color, so two layers of a similar shade are still two layers">
                <span>Pattern:</span>

                <div class="layerFillChoices" role="group" aria-label="Fill pattern">
                    @foreach (var choice in fillChoices())
                    {
                        <button type="button"
                                class="layerFillChoice @(fillOf(settingsFor) == choice.Fill ? "layerFillChoiceOn" : "")"
                                data-fill="@choice.Fill"
                                aria-pressed="@(fillOf(settingsFor) == choice.Fill ? "true" : "false")"
                                title="@choice.Name"
                                @onclick="() => fillChosen(choice.Fill)">
                            @*
                                slice, not the default meet: the tile is square and the button is not, so
                                fitting the whole viewBox inside leaves a bar of popup either side of every
                                swatch - which on Solid is a red stripe in a white box rather than a solid
                                square. Slice covers the button and crops, which is the right answer for a
                                texture and keeps a dot round where stretching would not.
                            *@
                            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid slice" aria-hidden="true">
                                @((MarkupString)patternTile(choice.Fill))
                            </svg>
                        </button>
                    }
                </div>
            </div>

            @*
                How big a repeat of it is, in pixels on the screen.

                **In pixels rather than in the file's units**, because the view holds a pattern at a constant
                screen size as the zoom changes - so the number that means anything to somebody looking at
                the drawing is the one at that end. A tile in database units would be a different size on
                every file, and a different size again at every zoom.

                Only where there is a pattern to size. On a solid layer this is a box whose number changes
                nothing, which reads as the control being broken rather than as not applying.
            *@
            @if (fillOf(settingsFor) != LayerFill.None)
            {
                @*
                    The unit is in the label rather than beside the box, unlike the grid pitch two panels
                    away: Height and Thickness above are the same shape of row and put theirs in the
                    tooltip, and a third arrangement in one popup is a popup that looks assembled.
                *@
                <label class="layerSettingsRow"
                       title="How big one repeat of the pattern is on screen, in pixels. It stays this size as you zoom in">
                    <span>Size (px):</span>

                    <input type="number"
                           id="patternSize"
                           class="layerSettingsStack"
                           min="@LayerNames.LeastPatternPixels"
                           max="@LayerNames.MostPatternPixels"
                           step="1"
                           value="@patternPixelsOf(settingsFor)"
                           @onchange="patternSizeTyped" />
                </label>
            }

            <hr class="layerSettingsRule" />

            @*
                Named, the way the pattern above it is.

                Everything higher up this popup is a labeled row - Name, Show Labels, Height, Thickness,
                Role, Pattern - and then the biggest control in it arrived with no word at all: a square of
                color, a slider and three numbered boxes, left to be recognized. It is recognizable, and it
                was still the one section a reader had to identify rather than read.
            *@
            @*
                And which of the layer's two colors it changes.

                **One picker, aimed.** A layer with a pattern has a color for the ground and a color for the
                marks over it, and a second square-slider-and-three-boxes for the second one would double the
                tallest section of the tallest popup in the app. The switch says which of them the control
                below is currently about, which is a row rather than a second control.

                Only where there are two. A solid layer has one color, and a toggle offering to change the
                other one is a control with nothing on the end of it.
            *@
            <div class="layerSettingsRow layerSettingsFills">
                <span>Color:</span>

                @if (canColorPattern())
                {
                    <div class="colorTargets" role="group" aria-label="Which color to change">
                        <button type="button"
                                id="colorBackground"
                                class="colorTarget @(colorTarget == ColorTarget.Background ? "colorTargetOn" : "")"
                                aria-pressed="@(colorTarget == ColorTarget.Background ? "true" : "false")"
                                title="Change the color the layer is filled with"
                                @onclick="() => aimColorAt(ColorTarget.Background)">Background</button>

                        <button type="button"
                                id="colorPattern"
                                class="colorTarget @(colorTarget == ColorTarget.Pattern ? "colorTargetOn" : "")"
                                aria-pressed="@(colorTarget == ColorTarget.Pattern ? "true" : "false")"
                                title="Change the color the pattern's marks are drawn in"
                                @onclick="() => aimColorAt(ColorTarget.Pattern)">Pattern</button>
                    </div>
                }
            </div>

            @*
                The picker itself, open, rather than a swatch that opens the operating system's one over
                the top of this. A native dialog covers the layout, which is the thing being recolored -
                and the whole reason this popup sits over the drawing instead of in the sidebar.

                Saturation across, value down, hue on the slider. The field's own background is the hue at
                full strength; the two gradients over it wash it out to white going left and down to black
                going down, which is the standard square and costs no images.
            *@
            @*
                mouseup and change are what put a color in the history, not the dragging - see
                colorSettled. mouseup sits on the field rather than the popup because a release outside
                the field is a drag that wandered off, and the color under the pointer when it left is
                not one anybody chose.
            *@
            <div class="layerSettingsField"
                 style="background-color: @hueColor();"
                 @onmousedown="fieldPressed"
                 @onmousemove="fieldDragged"
                 @onmouseup="colorSettled"
                 title="Drag to choose. Across for how strong the color is, down for how dark">
                @*
                    pointer-events are off it in the CSS. Without that, a press that lands on the marker
                    reports its offset within the marker rather than within the field, and the color jumps
                    to a corner the moment you try to nudge the one you already have.
                *@
                <span class="layerSettingsFieldMark"
                      style="left: @percent(pickedColor.Saturation); top: @percent(1 - pickedColor.Value);"></span>
            </div>

            @*oninput draws as it slides, onchange is the one that lands - so only the latter is kept.*@
            <input type="range" class="layerSettingsHue" min="0" max="360" step="1"
                   value="@pickedColor.Hue.ToString("F0", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)"
                   @oninput="hueChanged"
                   @onchange="colorSettled"
                   title="The hue, all the way round the wheel" />

            @*
                The same color as numbers, for when it is a number you have rather than a shade you are
                looking for - a value out of a layer properties file, or one being matched to another
                tool's.
            *@
            <div class="layerSettingsChannels">
                @foreach (var channel in rgbChannels())
                {
                    <label title="@channel.Name, 0 to 255">
                        <span>@channel.Letter</span>
                        <input type="number" min="0" max="255" step="1"
                               class="layerSettingsChannel"
                               value="@channel.Level"
                               @onchange="e => channelTyped(e, channel.Letter)" />
                    </label>
                }
            </div>

            @*
                The colors picked before this one, newest first, so a scheme can be applied across several
                layers without hunting the same shade out of a gradient each time.
            *@
            @if (recentColors.Count > 0)
            {
                <div class="layerSettingsHistoryTitle" title="Colors used recently, newest first. Click one to give this layer the same color">History</div>
                <div class="layerSettingsHistory">
                    @foreach (string recent in recentColors)
                    {
                        <button type="button"
                                class="layerSwatch"
                                style="background-color: @recent;"
                                title="Give this layer @recent"
                                @onclick="() => chooseRecentColor(recent)"></button>
                    }
                </div>
            }

            <div class="layerSettingsFooter">
                @*
                    What "put it back" means depends on which color the picker is on: the layer's own goes
                    back to its place on the gradient, and the pattern's goes back to following the layer.
                    One action rather than two, because only one of them is reachable at a time.
                *@
                @if (colorTarget == ColorTarget.Pattern)
                {
                    <span id="resetColor" class="layerSidebarAction" @onclick="resetColor"
                          title="Draw the pattern in the layer's own color again">Match the layer</span>
                }
                else
                {
                    <span id="resetColor" class="layerSidebarAction" @onclick="resetColor"
                          title="Give this layer back the color it was assigned from the gradient">Reset to palette</span>
                }

                @*Only when there is something to put back, the same way Clear History only appears with rows to clear.*@
                @if (stackIsCustom(settingsFor))
                {
                    <span class="layerSidebarAction" @onclick="resetStack" title="Put this layer back on the even spacing the view works out for itself">Reset stack</span>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    }


    @*The space under the viewer is padding on .content now, rather than three empty paragraphs.*@

</div>

@code {

    #region Variables ****************************************

    private IRenderable? viewer = null;

    ///<summary>
    ///What has been edited into the open file and what can be taken back.
